    "case_id": "4",
    "case_summary": "A female patient in her late 20s presented with a 2-year history of multiple, slowly growing, infiltrated erythematous-violaceous patches and plaques, with occasional slight pain extending from her left upper arm to the left side of her chest. No triggers such as local infection, insect bite, or trauma occurred before lesion appearance. She denied fever, weight loss, or fatigue.\n\nMultiple firm, nonscaly, purple erythematous patches and plaques with irregular borders on her left axilla, medial upper arm, and lateral thoracic region. No extracutaneous involvement was observed.\n\n- Histopathological examination:    - Thin-walled, irregularly branched blood vessels dispersed between thickened collagen bundles    - Endothelial cells were plump but displayed no significant cytologic atypia      - Immunohistochemistry results:    - Positive for: CD31, CD34, Ki-67 (<1%)    - Negative for: podoplanin (D2-40), human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8)      - Images:    Image A Title: Clinical presentation    Image A Description: Multiple nonscaly, erythematous-violaceous patches and plaques with irregular borders on the patient's left axilla, medial upper arm, and lateral thoracic region        Image B Title: Histopathological examination (hematoxylin-eosin)    Image B Description: Irregularly branched, thin-walled blood vessels dissecting through collagen bundles within the superficial to mid dermis        Image C Title: Detailed histopathology (hematoxylin-eosin)    Image C Description: Irregularly branched, thin-walled blood vessels with plump endothelial cells dispersed between thickened collagen bundles without signs of atypia        Image D Title: Immunohistochemistry    Image D Description: Endothelial cells positive for cluster of differentiation (CD) 31\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Epithelioid hemangioma\nB. Morpheaform hemangioma\nC. Microvenular hemangioma (MVH)\nD. Tufted angioma\n",
